+### Shared variables and secrets
+
+**Docker → Variables** (0.131.0+, `action.terminal`, table
+`docker_variables`) holds variables several stacks share - `TZ`,
+`SMTP_HOST`, a mail password. Each has a name (`[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*`,
+up to 128 characters), a value (one line, up to 16 KiB), an optional
+description and a **scope**: all machines, one machine group or one
+machine. For a stack's machine the most specific definition of a name
+wins - the machine's own, then its group's, then the one for all
+machines.
+
+- Every value is stored encrypted (`encrypt_secret`). A variable marked
+  **secret** is never shown again, on the page or in the API; editing it
+  keeps the value unless *Set a new value* is chosen, and it stays secret
+  until it gets a new value.
+- A stack **picks** variables by name (*Shared variables* on the stack
+  form, up to 100). Nothing reaches a stack that didn't pick it.
+- On every deploy and check the picked values for the stack's machine are
+  appended to the `.env` debcontrol writes, after a
+  `# Shared variables (debcontrol)` line, quoted so Compose takes them
+  literally. A variable the stack's own `.env` already sets keeps the
+  stack's value. A picked name defined nowhere for that machine is left
+  out, and the stack page says so.
+- The stack's content hash includes the shared values, so changing a
+  variable shows the stacks using it as *changed since deploy*; deploy
+  them to apply it. Stacks that pick no variable keep exactly the hash
+  they had before 0.131.0.
+- An account limited to some machine groups manages only its groups' and
+  machines' variables and sees the ones for all machines read-only (it
+  can still pick them: they apply to every machine by definition).
+
+Audited as `docker.variable.create` / `.edit` / `.delete` (never the
+value).
+
+### Taking over what already runs
+
+**Adopting a Compose project** (0.131.0+, *Adopt* on a project under
+*Compose projects on the machine*, `action.terminal`). The project's
+compose file and `.env` are read from the machine (with several compose
+files, `docker compose config --no-interpolate` merges them into one
+without filling in variables, so nothing from the environment leaks
+into it) and stored as a stack written in debcontrol with the same
+name - the Compose project name - and **the same directory**. The only
+change on the machine is the `.debcontrol-stack` mark in that directory;
+nothing restarts. The first deploy writes `compose.yaml` and `.env` next
+to the project's own files and recreates only what differs. Refused
+(nothing stored) when debcontrol already manages that name or directory,
+the directory is gone, isn't writable by debcontrol's account or is
+already marked for another stack, the directory is one debcontrol never
+deploys into (see *Directory* above), `docker compose config` fails, or
+the project is debcontrol's own. Audited as
+`machine.docker.project.adopt` (also a refusal, with its reason).
+
+**A `docker run` container to a stack** (0.131.0+, *Make a stack from it*
+on a container not started by Compose, `action.terminal`). debcontrol
+reads `docker container inspect` and the image's own settings and fills
+in the new-stack form:
+
+- image, `container_name`, restart policy, command / entrypoint / user /
+  working directory where they differ from the image, ports, bind mounts,
+  tmpfs, capabilities, devices, DNS, extra hosts, sysctls, memory / CPU
+  limits, shared memory, logging, labels (not the image's own) and a
+  health check the image doesn't already define;
+- environment variables the image doesn't set go into the stack's `.env`
+  (stored encrypted) and reach the container through `env_file: .env`,
+  never into the compose file;
+- named volumes and networks other than the default bridge become
+  `external`, so the data stays where it is; a container on the default
+  bridge keeps `network_mode: bridge`;
+- every `$` is doubled, so Compose doesn't interpolate a label holding a
+  password hash;
+- what has no equivalent (`--link`, `--volumes-from`, GPU requests) is
+  listed as a warning above the form.
+
+Review the file and save. The stack remembers the container
+(`replace_container`); its **first deploy** stops it and renames it to
+`<name>-pre-debcontrol`, runs `up`, and removes the old container once the
+stack runs (and is healthy, with automatic rollback on). If `up` or the
+health wait fails, the stack is taken down and the old container renamed
+back and started. A container that belongs to a Compose project, or
+debcontrol's own, is never touched.
+
+### Drift
+
+**Drift** (0.131.0+) is a managed stack changed on the machine outside
+debcontrol. *Check drift* on the stack page (`action.updates`; it only
+reads) compares the machine with what debcontrol deployed last:
+
+- the SHA-256 of `compose.yaml` (written in debcontrol) and `.env` in the
+  stack's directory against the deployed ones (changed or missing);
+- for a Git stack, the commit checked out in `repo/` (read from
+  `.git/HEAD`) against the deployed commit;
+- each container's `com.docker.compose.config-hash` label against
+  `docker compose config --hash` of the files there now: a container
+  created from other settings (edited files, `docker compose up` or
+  `docker run` by hand), a service without a container, or a container
+  of the project without a service.
+
+The result, its time and the findings are kept on the stack and shown
+on its page and, as a badge, in the stacks list. *Check for drift every
+hour* (off by default) adds the stack to an hourly sweep (Beat
+`check-stack-drift`, one task per stack); turning to drift sends the
+`docker.stack.drift` notification once. A stack that was never deployed
+by debcontrol (just adopted, say) isn't checked - there is nothing to
+compare with yet.
+
+To put it right, **deploy** (back to what is stored), or - for a stack
+written in debcontrol - **Take the machine's files over**
+(`action.terminal`): its `compose.yaml` and `.env` are read back and
+stored, without the shared variables debcontrol appended (they stay
+shared). Nothing is deployed. Audited as `machine.docker.stack.import`.
+
+## Private registries
+
+**Docker → Registries** (0.131.0+, `action.terminal`, table
+`docker_registries`) holds logins to private registries: a server
+(`ghcr.io`, `registry.gitlab.com`, `registry.example.com:5000`; entered
+with or without `https://`, stored as host[:port]), a user name, a
+password or token (stored encrypted, never shown again) and a scope - all
+machines, one group or one machine; for a machine the most specific
+login per server applies.
+
+- Saving one queues a login on every active machine in its scope that
+  has Docker and a pinned host key (one task per machine). The machine
+  runs `docker login --password-stdin`: the password travels on SSH
+  standard input only, never on a command line. Docker keeps the login in
+  the configuration of the account debcontrol connects as
+  (`~/.docker/config.json`, or a credential helper set up there), so
+  pulls, stack deploys and image update checks all use it.
+- Each machine's **image update check** logs in first wherever a login
+  is missing, failed, or older than the last change of its entry, so a
+  machine added to a group later picks its logins up by itself. Servers
+  no entry applies to any more are logged out.
+- The page shows per registry how many machines are logged in and which
+  failed, with docker's message. *Log in again* forces a new login
+  everywhere in scope (after rotating a token). Removing an entry logs
+  the machines out (or over to a broader entry for the same server).
+- An account limited to some machine groups manages only its groups' and
+  machines' logins and sees the ones for all machines read-only, with the
+  login results of its own machines only.
+- Changing an entry's server needs the password again: a password is never
+  sent to another server than it was given for.
+
+Audited as `docker.registry.create` / `.edit` / `.login` / `.delete`
+(never the password).
+
